WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.513
Welcome to Building Azure Security,
Chapter 6: Azure Firewall and egress.

2
00:00:05.513 --> 00:00:08.238
A firewall nobody routes
to inspects nothing.

3
00:00:08.238 --> 00:00:11.344
So the kit reads the policy
and the routes together,

4
00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:16.300
and it admits what it can't see: the effective
route on a running network interface.

5
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.466
We'll start from Microsoft
Learn's Zero Trust checks,

6
00:00:19.466 --> 00:00:24.178
route commands and Draft and Deployment
procedure, see what they leave to you,

7
00:00:24.178 --> 00:00:27.565
read the kit's check and the
plan it writes but never runs,

8
00:00:27.565 --> 00:00:30.437
then walk two labs, the
evidence and the dangers.

9
00:00:30.437 --> 00:00:33.453
The chapter is a draft
awaiting the owner's review,

10
00:00:33.453 --> 00:00:37.720
and the code is illustrative,
tested against a fictional estate.

11
00:00:41.470 --> 00:00:44.008
Why read the policy and
the routes together?

12
00:00:44.008 --> 00:00:48.540
Because a policy that denies inspects
nothing if workloads don't route to it,

13
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.282
and a route to a firewall in alert
mode lets everything through.

14
00:00:52.282 --> 00:00:56.674
By the end of this chapter you can say
whether every firewall policy denies on

15
00:00:56.674 --> 00:00:59.350
threat intelligence and
intrusion detection,

16
00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:03.810
whether each firewall has a policy and
logs, and what share of workload subnets

17
00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:05.938
declare a default route to a firewall,

18
00:01:05.938 --> 00:01:08.545
which is the guide's
egress-inspection metric.

19
00:01:08.545 --> 00:01:13.620
And you can hand the platform owner the
commands that move a policy to deny, unrun.

20
00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.750
It's for platform owners, security monitors,
and the developers who review both.

21
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.375
Here's Microsoft's starting point.

22
00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:29.353
Learn's Zero Trust recommendations for
Azure Firewall check that outbound traffic

23
00:01:29.353 --> 00:01:31.985
from workloads routes
through the firewall,

24
00:01:31.985 --> 00:01:36.252
that threat intelligence and intrusion
detection are in alert and deny,

25
00:01:36.252 --> 00:01:39.950
that outbound TLS is inspected,
and that diagnostic logs are on.

26
00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:43.361
Threat intelligence deny
needs Standard or Premium;

27
00:01:43.361 --> 00:01:46.850
intrusion detection and TLS
inspection need Premium.

28
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.850
The CLI sets each mode
to alert, deny or off.

29
00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:54.988
Learn's route commands add a default
route to the firewall's private IP.

30
00:01:54.988 --> 00:01:59.050
And Learn's Draft and Deployment
procedure stages every change in

31
00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:03.410
a draft and deploys it at once,
replacing the whole applied policy.

32
00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:08.523
What does Learn leave to you?

33
00:02:08.523 --> 00:02:13.611
The guide's Validate query reads one
policy, and policies drift one at a time.

34
00:02:13.611 --> 00:02:16.637
A route command per subnet
means a subnet added

35
00:02:16.637 --> 00:02:19.198
later has no route, or
one to the internet.

36
00:02:19.198 --> 00:02:23.185
A virtual appliance at some address
may not be the firewall at all.

37
00:02:23.185 --> 00:02:27.985
A Standard policy can't deny on intrusion
detection whatever its mode says.

38
00:02:27.985 --> 00:02:30.672
Sent by automation, a draft would deploy

39
00:02:30.672 --> 00:02:34.010
without review and replace
live edits made since.

40
00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:38.160
And the route a machine actually uses
can include system routes that win.

41
00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.290
The kit answers each one, and is honest
that it reads declared routes only.

42
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.865
Here's how the kit reads egress.

43
00:02:48.865 --> 00:02:53.464
It takes every subnet in the workload
subscriptions the settings name,

44
00:02:53.464 --> 00:02:56.504
except the reserved ones:
the gateway, firewall,

45
00:02:56.504 --> 00:03:00.090
firewall management, Bastion
and route server subnets.

46
00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:01.427
Each gets one state.

47
00:03:01.427 --> 00:03:05.616
Firewall, when the default route
goes to a firewall's private IP.

48
00:03:05.616 --> 00:03:09.340
Other appliance, when it goes to
an address the kit doesn't know.

49
00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.827
No route table, or no default route.

50
00:03:11.827 --> 00:03:14.914
Or the next hop type it
found, such as the internet.

51
00:03:14.914 --> 00:03:18.265
The guide's egress-inspection
metric is the firewall

52
00:03:18.265 --> 00:03:21.970
subnets over all workload
subnets, by declared route.

53
00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:27.807
The check reports seven rules.

54
00:03:27.807 --> 00:03:32.422
Threat intelligence not in deny, and
intrusion detection not in deny or a

55
00:03:32.422 --> 00:03:37.421
policy that isn't Premium, are findings
straight from Learn's Zero Trust checks.

56
00:03:37.421 --> 00:03:41.733
A workload subnet without a default
route to the firewall is a finding;

57
00:03:41.733 --> 00:03:44.633
one routed to an unknown
appliance is a warning.

58
00:03:44.633 --> 00:03:47.295
A firewall with no logs is a finding.

59
00:03:47.295 --> 00:03:52.124
A firewall with no policy, and a
Premium policy with no TLS inspection,

60
00:03:52.124 --> 00:03:57.545
are warnings: the program pilots TLS
inspection on one rule collection first.

61
00:03:57.545 --> 00:04:01.422
And for each policy not yet in
deny, the kit writes Learn's four

62
00:04:01.422 --> 00:04:05.880
draft and deployment commands to
a plan file, and never runs them.

63
00:04:09.630 --> 00:04:10.680
Who's involved?

64
00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:14.464
The platform owner owns the
firewall policy and the routes,

65
00:04:14.464 --> 00:04:17.630
and runs the draft in a
change window, after review.

66
00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:20.768
The security monitor reads
the threat intelligence

67
00:04:20.768 --> 00:04:23.692
and intrusion detection
logs the firewall sends.

68
00:04:23.692 --> 00:04:28.105
And developers review the check, the
plan file and the routes it reads.

69
00:04:28.105 --> 00:04:33.180
Every read the check makes is Reader;
the plan is the platform owner's to run.

70
00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:42.230
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7 with Pester, in
the kit's folder, and no subscription.

71
00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:45.745
Contoso's hub firewall has
the private IP ten dot zero

72
00:04:45.745 --> 00:04:49.180
dot zero dot four and a
Premium policy still in alert.

73
00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:52.580
Two workload networks hold
five workload subnets.

74
00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.638
Step 1 runs the firewall check:

75
00:04:54.638 --> 00:04:59.330
two of five subnets route to the
firewall, and the plan file is written.

76
00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:01.630
Step 2 reads each subnet.

77
00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:04.492
The payroll data subnet
has no route table.

78
00:05:04.492 --> 00:05:08.381
The portal web subnet sends its
default route to the internet.

79
00:05:08.381 --> 00:05:12.152
And the jump subnet sends it to
ten dot nine dot nine dot nine,

80
00:05:12.152 --> 00:05:14.600
an appliance the kit doesn't know.

81
00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:23.923
Step 3 reads the findings: threat
intelligence and intrusion detection both in

82
00:05:23.923 --> 00:05:28.398
alert, no firewall logs, two subnets
with no route to the firewall,

83
00:05:28.398 --> 00:05:31.438
an unknown appliance,
and no TLS inspection.

84
00:05:31.438 --> 00:05:34.274
Step 4 reads the plan the
kit wrote and didn't run:

85
00:05:34.274 --> 00:05:37.984
Learn's four draft and deployment
commands for the hub policy,

86
00:05:37.984 --> 00:05:42.203
with its resource group and subscription,
under a header that says the

87
00:05:42.203 --> 00:05:46.349
kit did not run them, and points at
the guide's Step 6 and a change window.

88
00:05:46.349 --> 00:05:49.420
Step 5 runs this chapter's two tests.

89
00:05:53.170 --> 00:05:56.968
Lab 2 moves to a test subscription
with an Azure Firewall,

90
00:05:56.968 --> 00:06:01.332
its policy and a pilot spoke subnet
routed to it, never production.

91
00:06:01.332 --> 00:06:05.984
Sign in to commercial Azure with the
Resource Graph and firewall extensions,

92
00:06:05.984 --> 00:06:10.271
write the settings, and run the check
live, allowing the unverified calls.

93
00:06:10.271 --> 00:06:14.556
Then compare with the guide's Validate
commands: the policy's two modes,

94
00:06:14.556 --> 00:06:18.620
and the effective route table on a
test machine in the pilot subnet.

95
00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:20.804
For a subnet the kit called firewall,

96
00:06:20.804 --> 00:06:24.808
the effective default route should
go to the firewall's private IP.

97
00:06:24.808 --> 00:06:26.671
If it doesn't, record why.

98
00:06:26.671 --> 00:06:29.613
Nothing changed in Azure,
and the plan wasn't run,

99
00:06:29.613 --> 00:06:33.070
so back out by deleting
the output and signing out.

100
00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:39.485
Each output is evidence
for something specific,

101
00:06:39.485 --> 00:06:44.882
from the guide's requirements
table against NIST SP 800-53.

102
00:06:44.882 --> 00:06:49.360
Threat intelligence and intrusion
detection in deny on every policy

103
00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:53.683
supports deny by default, allow by
exception, and system monitoring.

104
00:06:53.683 --> 00:06:57.034
Every workload subnet's
declared route to the firewall,

105
00:06:57.034 --> 00:07:00.458
and the share that has one,
supports boundary protection.

106
00:07:00.458 --> 00:07:04.745
Firewall logs to the platform
workspace support system monitoring.

107
00:07:04.745 --> 00:07:07.902
And the move to deny, reviewed
before it's deployed,

108
00:07:07.902 --> 00:07:11.445
supports deny by default too,
through the change record.

109
00:07:11.445 --> 00:07:15.495
The kit's output supports these
controls; the assessor decides.

110
00:07:15.495 --> 00:07:20.076
The key security indicators are
FedRAMP's indicators for cloud providers,

111
00:07:20.076 --> 00:07:23.320
which the agency lines
its own evidence up with.

112
00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:28.470
What breaks in production?

113
00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:31.529
A draft deployed over newer
live edits loses them,

114
00:07:31.529 --> 00:07:36.230
because deploying replaces the whole
applied policy; the kit never deploys,

115
00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:40.558
and the plan names the change window
and the guide's single change path.

116
00:07:40.558 --> 00:07:44.783
A declared route that doesn't win
sends traffic around the firewall;

117
00:07:44.783 --> 00:07:48.632
the metric says declared, and
Lab 2 checks the effective route.

118
00:07:48.632 --> 00:07:51.852
A route to an unknown
appliance sends egress through

119
00:07:51.852 --> 00:07:55.583
something that isn't the inspecting
firewall; the kit names it.

120
00:07:55.583 --> 00:07:59.314
And a Standard policy with
intrusion detection set reads as

121
00:07:59.314 --> 00:08:03.630
configured but inspects nothing;
the kit says Premium is needed.

122
00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:08.730
Here's what done looks like.

123
00:08:08.730 --> 00:08:11.863
Every policy denies on
threat intelligence and

124
00:08:11.863 --> 00:08:14.605
intrusion detection, or
has a dated plan to.

125
00:08:14.605 --> 00:08:19.068
Every workload subnet routes to the
firewall, or has a decision record.

126
00:08:19.068 --> 00:08:22.981
The effective route was checked on
a machine in each pilot subnet.

127
00:08:22.981 --> 00:08:26.567
And the plan file went through the
change record before anyone ran it.

128
00:08:26.567 --> 00:08:30.243
Next, Chapter 7: platform
services reached only through

129
00:08:30.243 --> 00:08:33.590
private endpoints, and the
key vaults behind them.
